.TH auto-restore-user 8

\" auto-man2man (used in generating subcommand lists) depends on this section
.SH NAME
.PP
auto-restore-user - Restore a user account from old system files

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-restore-user user passwd-file shadow-file group-file
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
user        The username of the account to restore
passwd-file The old /etc/passwd file from before the reinstall
shadow-file The old encrypted passwd file from before the reinstall
group-file  The old group file from before the reinstall
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-restore-user
command restores a user account from old password and group files.
This is useful after an operating system reinstallation to selectively
reactivate users with the same passwords they had before.

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-restore-user joeuser passwd.old master.passwd.old group.old
.ad
.fi

.SH FILES
.nf
.na
/etc/passwd, /etc/master.passwd, /etc/shadow, /etc/group
.ad
.fi

.SH SEE ALSO

auto-admin(8), auto-restore-pw(8), auto-restore-ownership(8)

